---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import aps from '../assets/teaching/jack_at_aps.jpg';
import qaoa from '../assets/research/qaoa_mvpo.png';
import qmp from '../assets/research/qmp_washington.png';
import qvr from '../assets/research/qvr_loop.png';
import flux from '../assets/research/flux_closure_pto_sto.png';
import scaling from '../assets/research/scaling_behaviour.png';
import soft from '../assets/research/softdispersion.png';
import vortex from '../assets/research/vortex_pzo.png';

const sections = [
  { id: 'quantum', label: 'Quantum algorithms' },
  { id: 'ml', label: 'Machine learning' },
  { id: 'materials', label: 'Materials from first principles' },
];
---
<Base title="Research" image="/og/page/research.png" description="Quantum algorithms for chemistry and materials, machine learning for electronic structure, and large-scale first-principles simulation of ferroelectrics and antiferroelectrics.">
  <PageHead
    kicker="Research"
    title="Where quantum theory meets computation"
    lead="If quantum theory can be put to good use in a field, I want to work on it. In practice that means algorithms for quantum computers, machine learning for electronic structure, and first-principles simulation of materials at very large scale."
  >
    <nav class="jump rise" style="--i:3" aria-label="On this page">
      {sections.map((s) => <a class="chip" href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a>)}
    </nav>
  </PageHead>

  <section id="quantum" class="section wrap block">
    <div class="block-head">
      <p class="t-label kicker">01 · Quantum algorithms</p>
      <h2 class="t-h2">Simulating chemistry and materials on quantum computers</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cols">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Most of my time now goes on algorithms for early fault-tolerant quantum computers, with molecules and
          quantum materials as the target. In first-quantised simulation I have worked on
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07278">universal initial-state preparation</a> for fermions, bosons and
          paraparticles, and on <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.25029">circuits that convert coherently between first- and second-quantised representations</a>.
          For materials, I have combined quantum defect embedding with block-encoding and phase estimation to
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevA.110.032606">compute the optical properties of spin defects</a>,
          proposed algorithms that <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/x8gd-fbtr">screen defects for ODMR activity</a>, and
          used a quantum computer to <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22091">model the long-time dynamics of the NV centre in diamond</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Beyond chemistry, I am working on quantum algorithms for computational fluid dynamics as a visiting scientist in
          <a href="https://www.matter.toronto.edu/">Alán Aspuru-Guzik’s Matter Lab</a> at the University of Toronto.
        </p>
        <p>
          Results on today’s hardware still need care. With colleagues at LG I developed
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1088/2058-9565/ae5555">physics-inspired extrapolation</a>, a low-overhead error-mitigation
          protocol whose fitted slope also measures hardware noise, demonstrated on 84-qubit IBM quantum dynamics.
          The same toolkit made possible <a href="https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jctc.5c01911">multicomponent simulations beyond the Born–Oppenheimer approximation</a>
          on IBM Heron hardware, with ground-state energies within chemical accuracy.
        </p>
        <h3>Near-term algorithms</h3>
        <p>
          Before fault tolerance, the question is what a limited number of noisy qubits can usefully do. I have built
          quantum machine-learning methods for time-series classification, anomaly detection and outlier detection, using
          hybrid quantum–classical architectures and multiple-kernel learning, and I care about the open problems that stand
          in the way: barren plateaus, efficient training and generalisation.
        </p>
        <p>
          I have also benchmarked approximate optimisation (QAOA and quantum annealing) on superconducting and trapped-ion
          hardware for portfolio optimisation, using <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.06782">Wasserstein distances</a> to judge
          solution quality. Algorithm-agnostic tricks matter too: quantum multi-programming runs many circuits in parallel
          across a large chip, much as MPI distributes classical work, and error mitigation such as dynamical decoupling and
          Pauli twirling improves application-level results.
        </p>
        <p><a class="arrow-link" href="/publications/?topic=quantum-algorithms"><span class="lbl">Related publications</span><span class="arr">→</span></a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="figs">
        <Figure src={aps} alt="Jack S. Baker presenting on quantum machine learning at the APS March Meeting" photo width={520}>APS March Meeting 2023, Las Vegas, Nevada.</Figure>
        <Figure src={qaoa} alt="Schematic of the quantum approximate optimisation algorithm applied to portfolio optimisation" width={520}>The quantum approximate optimisation algorithm applied to portfolio optimisation.</Figure>
        <Figure src={qmp} alt="Qubit layout of parallel circuits on the 127-qubit ibm_washington processor" width={520}>Many circuits run in parallel across the 127-qubit transmon processor <i>ibm_washington</i>.</Figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="ml" class="section wrap block">
    <div class="block-head">
      <p class="t-label kicker">02 · Machine learning</p>
      <h2 class="t-h2">Learning where physics is expensive</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cols">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Density functional theory lives or dies by its exchange–correlation functional. At Xanadu I co-developed
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0181037">GradDFT</a>, a JAX library for designing neural functionals: fully
          differentiable, just-in-time compiled self-consistent-field loops, direct optimisation of Kohn–Sham orbitals, and
          many of the known constraints of the exact functional built in.
        </p>
        <p>
          On the quantum side, I have used time-evolution operators to build a trainable, time-dependent inner product for
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s42484-024-00149-0">kernelised time-series classification</a> on 127-qubit hardware,
          and <a href="https://doi.org/10.1609/aaaiss.v7i1.36902">Quantum Variational Rewinding</a> for anomaly detection,
          where error mitigation reduced anomaly-score errors by up to 14% on average.
        </p>
        <p>
          More broadly, I am interested in what increasingly capable AI will mean for how computational science gets done,
          from learned models of electronic structure to automated searches for better algorithms.
        </p>
        <p><a class="arrow-link" href="/publications/?topic=ml-chemistry"><span class="lbl">Related publications</span><span class="arr">→</span></a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="figs">
        <Figure src={qvr} alt="The training loop of Quantum Variational Rewinding, alternating classical and quantum processing" width={420}>Quantum Variational Rewinding: a classical optimiser trains time-devolution circuits run on a QPU.</Figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="materials" class="section wrap block">
    <div class="block-head">
      <p class="t-label kicker">03 · Materials</p>
      <h2 class="t-h2">Ferroics from first principles, at scale</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cols">
      <div class="prose">
        <h3 id="textures">Polar textures</h3>
        <p>
          A polar morphology is a pattern of electric dipoles in a solid, and some of them (polar vortices, skyrmions, waves
          and chiral bubbles) bring properties such as negative capacitance and giant electromechanical response. Capturing
          them atom by atom needs thousands to millions of atoms, beyond standard DFT. With the linear-scaling code
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0005074">CONQUEST</a> on national supercomputers, I simulated these textures directly.
        </p>
        <p>
          That work uncovered the mechanism behind <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/adts.202000154">polar waves in thin PbTiO<sub>3</sub> films</a>,
          critical thicknesses for flux-closure domains, and <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.127.247601">why ferroelectric domain walls align with surface trenches</a>:
          dipoles arrange themselves to minimise trench-induced depolarising fields, a mechanism that can be used to engineer
          textures in other nanostructures.
        </p>
        <h3 id="afe">Phase transitions in antiferroelectrics</h3>
        <p>
          Seventy years after antiferroelectricity was found in PbZrO<sub>3</sub>, there is still no agreed mechanism for the
          transition, nor even a settled definition. Much of my work here studies phonons: imaginary modes signal dynamical
          instabilities whose displacement patterns point to lower-symmetry phases. Following long-wavelength instabilities in
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.100.224305">PZT</a> and PbZrO<sub>3</sub>, we found
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.08856">structures of PbZrO<sub>3</sub> and PbHfO<sub>3</sub> lower in energy</a> than the accepted ground state.
        </p>
        <p>
          Why many of these structures are not seen in experiment is open. Quantum nuclear effects that suppress certain modes,
          and strain gradients that stabilise exotic patterns, are two candidates; answering them may need million-atom molecular
          dynamics with machine-learned interatomic potentials.
        </p>
        <p><a class="arrow-link" href="/publications/?topic=materials"><span class="lbl">Related publications</span><span class="arr">→</span></a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="figs">
        <Figure src={flux} alt="Polar vortex–antivortex pairs in PbTiO3 films on SrTiO3" width={520}>Polar vortex–antivortex pairs in PbTiO<sub>3</sub> films on SrTiO<sub>3</sub>, from first-principles atomic structures.</Figure>
        <Figure src={scaling} alt="Strong, linear and weak scaling of the CONQUEST code up to 200,000 cores" width={520}>CONQUEST scaling with PbTiO<sub>3</sub>: (a) strong scaling, (b) linear scaling with system size, (c) weak scaling to 200,000 cores.</Figure>
        <Figure src={soft} alt="Phonon dispersions of PbZrO3 and PbHfO3 showing unstable modes" width={520}>Unstable phonons in PbZrO<sub>3</sub> and PbHfO<sub>3</sub>: (a) cubic paraelectric; (b) antiferroelectric <i>Pbam</i>, with an instability remaining near Z.</Figure>
        <Figure src={vortex} alt="Dipolar vortices in antiferroelectric PbZrO3" width={420}>Dipolar vortices in antiferroelectric PbZrO<sub>3</sub> and PbHfO<sub>3</sub>.</Figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .jump { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-2); margin-top: var(--space-2); }
  .block { scroll-margin-top: 80px; }
  .block-head { display: grid; gap: var(--space-3); margin-bottom: var(--space-8); padding-top: var(--space-8); border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .cols { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(280px, 400px); gap: var(--space-12); align-items: start; }
  .figs { display: grid; gap: var(--space-8); }
  .prose h3 { scroll-margin-top: 88px; }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .cols { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .figs { position: static; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); } }
</style>
